-- | The OBSERVABLE `emit` SigOp — a program's effect trace, made visible.
--
-- Once has no `print`: the only other effectful SigOps are syscalls, and `exit`
-- terminates. `emit` is the language's way to say something WITHOUT halting, and
-- that is what makes an effect trace observable at runtime rather than only at
-- the Agda `obs` level.
--
-- D220: this used to be a NOP — a bare `ret` — with a header declaring it "NOT a
-- real Strata interpretation". That was the defect. An interpretation whose
-- runtime does not do what its name says cannot witness anything, and because
-- every `.once` harness resolves `I.Test.Emit` from HERE, it meant no exit test
-- could tell "emitted, then exited 7" from "exited 7". Six tests claimed effects
-- they never performed, and one of them was hiding a real codegen bug (D221).
-- The byte-writing implementation already existed, in a duplicate strata root
-- used by `TraceSpec` alone; this is that implementation, promoted to be the
-- only one.
signature emit : Eff Int Unit

-- | The same for a `Float`, and observable for the same reason.
--
-- BOTH write the argument's WHOLE MACHINE WORD, little-endian — 8 bytes on a
-- 64-bit target, 4 on x86-32. A test that reads those bytes observes exactly
-- what D114 says the trace carries: the SigOp's argument at its own base type,
-- not a projection of it.
--
-- `emit` used to write only the low byte, which made `emit 42` and `emit 298`
-- the same trace — a bug corrupting an argument's high bits would have passed.
-- That is the same defect D114 found in the spec, one layer down.
--
-- The two implementations are IDENTICAL on a 64-bit target, which is D113
-- showing through: at the machine level an `Int` argument and a `Float` argument
-- are both one machine word. What differs is the SOURCE type, and hence what the
-- word MEANS — `42` against `0x3FE0000000000000` for `0.5` at binary64.
signature emitF : Eff Float Unit
